//! Routines whose questions sit inside one another.
//!
//! 9160 of them, read as a tree rather than as a list. Every earlier sweep of the
//! branching routines wanted the questions at the top level and threw away
//! anything with one question inside another; this one walks the braces, so a
//! step carries how deep it sits along with what it asks and what it calls.
//!
//! Reading it as a tree is what makes it honest. A statement that follows a
//! closed branch is not part of that branch — it runs whatever the answer was —
//! and it is recorded here as [`Does::Straightaway`] at the depth it actually
//! sits at, not folded into the question above it. For the same reason an
//! `else if` is [`Does::AskingOtherwise`] rather than a plain question: that it
//! is reached only because the questions before it failed is part of what it
//! means. 293 routines ask two or more of those in a row, which is a value tried
//! against one constant after another — a decision over cases written by hand.
//!
//! Two things come out of the shape.
//!
//! The nesting is shallow and it thins fast. 3753 of the 9160 have anything inside
//! anything at all, and only 745 go two deep or further. The work stays at the
//! top: a step at the outermost level calls about one and a sixth things on
//! average, a step below that about three quarters of one. What the nesting
//! does is narrow a condition, not carry the routine.
//!
//! And 946 of them are constructors — the run time's make-an-instance together
//! with its after-it-is-made — which in **every one of the 946**, without an
//! exception, sit in different steps. That is the same thing
//! [`crate::question_routines`] found in the routines whose questions were all
//! at the top level, holding here in a population it never looked at. Each of
//! the two is called exactly once per constructor.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
#![allow(clippy::unreadable_literal)]

pub use crate::guarded_routines::Asks;
pub use crate::question_routines::{AFTER_IT_IS_MADE, MAKES_AN_INSTANCE, TAKES_IT_APART};

/// What one step of a routine is.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Does {
    /// Statements that run whatever the answers were.
    Straightaway,
    /// A loop.
    Repeatedly,
    /// A loop whose body was recovered as running at least once: a do-loop.
    ///
    /// Which says how the loop was rendered rather than how it was written — a
    /// counted loop the compiler knew would run at least once is printed this
    /// way whichever way it was typed. Used by [`crate::looping_routines`].
    RepeatedlyAtLeastOnce,
    /// A question, and the branch it opens.
    Asking(Asks),
    /// A question asked only because every question before it failed.
    ///
    /// An `else if`. Recorded apart from a plain question because being reached
    /// at all is part of what the branch means: the conditions before it are
    /// known to have failed, which a bare question does not say.
    AskingOtherwise(Asks),
}

/// One step: how deep it sits, what it is, and what it calls.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct Step {
    /// How many branches enclose it. Zero is the body of the routine itself.
    pub depth: u8,
    /// What the step is.
    pub does: Does,
    /// What its own statements call, in order.
    pub calls: &'static [u32],
}

/// One routine read as a tree.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct Routine {
    /// Where it sits.
    pub routine: u32,
    /// Its steps, in the order they are written.
    pub steps: &'static [Step],
    /// Whether it also calls through a thunk the decompiler could not resolve.
    ///
    /// Such a call has no recovered function behind it, so it is not in any
    /// [`Step::calls`]; this says the routine makes one anyway, so the call
    /// lists are known to be short of it rather than silently wrong.
    pub through_a_thunk: bool,
}

/// The lowest address any recovered function sits at.
pub const FIRST_RECOVERED: u32 = 0x00406DE0;

/// The highest address any recovered function sits at.
pub const LAST_RECOVERED: u32 = 0x01D87790;

